WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.713
Step two chooses one approver
system per authority class.

2
00:00:03.713 --> 00:00:06.218
The guide's example, design guidance:

3
00:00:06.218 --> 00:00:11.062
ServiceNow approves A zero and A one
through its Ask for Approval flow.

4
00:00:11.062 --> 00:00:13.527
SailPoint approves A two and A three,

5
00:00:13.527 --> 00:00:18.787
through access-request approvals by an
owner, a manager or a governance group.

6
00:00:18.787 --> 00:00:23.301
The Service Catalog app only
collects A two and A three requests.

7
00:00:23.301 --> 00:00:28.125
And every approval's timeout outcome
is reject or cancel, never approve.

8
00:00:28.125 --> 00:00:33.300
Step three gives each change class
a lane, from Book 13's Appendix B.

9
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.512
The mapping onto these products
is the program's inference.

10
00:00:36.512 --> 00:00:39.428
An autonomous class runs
as a standard change,

11
00:00:39.428 --> 00:00:43.263
and the authorizing official
accepts any class run that way.

12
00:00:43.263 --> 00:00:46.551
A gated class is an access
request with approval.

13
00:00:46.551 --> 00:00:50.710
Step four writes the decisions to
the record as a reviewed change.
